(a) The United States Surgeon General has stated that smoking of cigarettes and the use of tobacco products is the leading cause of premature death and disability in the United States of America. Medical evidence confirms that smoking of tobacco can be hazardous to the health of the smoker and nonsmokers. Medical research has confirmed that nonsmokers with chronic heart or lung disease can experience distress when exposed to sufficient concentrations of secondhand smoke. Many allergic individuals and healthy nonsmokers report discomfort when exposed to secondhand smoke. Recent medical studies provide sufficient evidence that long-term exposure to involuntary smoking may increase a nonsmoker's risk of developing serious lung disease.
(b) The city safety committee has reviewed and has recommended the adoption of smoking prohibitions for buildings owned, leased or rented by the city, as well as vehicles owned or leased and operated by employees of the city.
(c) The mayor and council find that it is in the best interest of the health, welfare and safety of the city and its citizens:
(1) To provide a healthy, comfortable and productive working environment for its employees in city public buildings and facilities serving the public, as well as in publicly owned motor vehicles;
(2) To protect nonsmokers;
(3) To assist employees in adjusting to restrictions on smoking and the use of tobacco products;
(4) To prohibit smoking of cigarettes, cigars and the use of other tobacco products in any city owned, leased or rented enclosed building space, as well as any motor vehicle or equipment owned or leased by the city.
(Ord. No. 773, § 59-1, 12-8-2003)

